{"kind":"report","text":"Approved exact test-only head 7e3235210637b5a3edea2cd161d0050ea05932a6, internal/service/wait_filter_test.go. No blocking findings.\n\nArchitecture: layer 7 service tests change; no production code, interface, fold, timeout policy or architecture contract changes. docs/reference/architecture.md@7e3235210637b5a3edea2cd161d0050ea05932a6 needs no amendment.\n\nSecurity: append helpers that may call t.Fatal now run synchronously on the test goroutine. The only new worker runs the poll and sends its result on a buffered channel; it reports no test failure, and the main success path receives the result. On an early failure it cannot block forever trying to send. The repaired test requires each append to finish before accepting a poll answer, so a loaded machine fails inconclusively instead of passing without exercising three unrelated acts. No production trust or authority boundary changes and no unresolved security defect found.\n\nSimplification: swapping which side runs in the worker removes the unsafe appending worker entirely, making an additional append join or shared error collector unnecessary. The existing named test remains a direct check of unrelated-event filtering. No extra mechanism to remove.\n\nIndependent verification: go test -race -timeout 10m ./internal/service -run '^TestActionablePollStillDeclinesAfterSeveralUnrelatedEvents$' -count=5 passed (36.713 s); git diff --check passed and the exact checkout is clean. I read the author report's full-suite claim rather than rerunning unaffected packages.\n\nCompiling adversarial control: shorten only this test's poll to 500 ms while retaining three 300 ms append gaps. The named test fails with the new 'poll answered ... before unrelated event 2 of 3 arrived' check. Remove that check under the same early-poll setup and the named test passes vacuously. This distinguishes guard coverage from an ordinary passing timing run. Source review proves the lifetime repair: no append helper remains